WEBVTT

00:00:00.018 --> 00:00:05.007
COUNT can answer different questions, so it is important to choose the right version.

00:00:05.007 --> 00:00:08.072
COUNT star counts input rows.

00:00:08.072 --> 00:00:14.512
It does not care whether individual columns contain missing values.

00:00:14.512 --> 00:00:18.617
COUNT of a specific column counts only rows where that expression is not NULL.

00:00:18.617 --> 00:00:23.007
COUNT DISTINCT counts unique non-null values.

00:00:23.007 --> 00:00:26.052
Now run the example in the browser.

00:00:26.052 --> 00:00:28.567
Compare the three result columns.

00:00:28.567 --> 00:00:32.327
The total admission count should include every admission row.

00:00:32.327 --> 00:00:38.582
The completed-admission count should be smaller because ongoing admissions have no discharge time.

00:00:38.582 --> 00:00:45.322
The distinct patient count answers a different question again: how many unique patients appear.

00:00:45.322 --> 00:00:52.342
If you are using pgAdmin, copy the query into the Query Tool and run the same comparison there.

00:00:52.342 --> 00:00:57.902
Look at the labels carefully and connect each number to the question it answers.

00:00:57.902 --> 00:01:03.207
Always name the thing being counted or averaged.

00:01:03.207 --> 00:01:07.732
COUNT star counts rows, COUNT of a column ignores NULL in that column,

00:01:07.732 --> 00:01:11.427
and COUNT DISTINCT answers a different question again.

00:01:11.427 --> 00:01:19.212
In dashboards, a vague label such as Count can hide major analytical errors; use labels such as admissions,

00:01:19.212 --> 00:01:21.496
patients, or distinct orders.
